---
title: RODE Solvers on Biological Models
author: Harsh Singh
---

Chapter 15 of Han and Kloeden, *Random Ordinary Differential Equations and Their Numerical
Solution* ([Springer 2017](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-10-6265-0)), tests RODE schemes on
four models from the life sciences. This page solves three of them with three RODE solvers of
StochasticDiffEq.jl, `RandomEM`, `RandomHeun` and `RandomTaylor15`, and for contrast with the
classical `Euler`, `Tsit5` and `Vern9` on the same paths. The fourth model, the sea shell
pattern model of Section 15.4, is a system of partial differential equations and is omitted.

The noise is of a different kind in each model: an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process inside a cosine,
two compound Poisson processes added to the activation terms, and a Wiener process inside a
bounded rational function. For each model the page measures the strong convergence of every
solver, and at the end the error against time for one path.

```julia
using StochasticDiffEq, DiffEqNoiseProcess, OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5,
      OrdinaryDiffEqVerner, BenchmarkTools, Plots, Random, Statistics, Printf
gr()

const SEED = 20261004
const FINE = 2^20
const NSAVE = 2^14
const STEPS = [32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]
const WP_STEPS = [64, 256, 1024, 4096, 16384]
const PATHS = 16
```

## Noise paths and reference solution

Each noise path is generated once on a grid of $2^{20}$ cells from the exact transition of its
process, so the stored values are samples of the process itself: Gaussian increments for the
Wiener process, the Gaussian transition of the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process with the variance
derived in Section 14.3 of the book, and exponential waiting times with uniform jump sizes for
the compound Poisson process of Section 14.5.

Every solver sees a stored path as the piecewise linear function through its points. The RODE
solvers read it from a `NoiseGrid`, which interpolates linearly, and the ODE solvers call the
interpolant `make_W` from their right-hand side. A jump of a compound Poisson process therefore
appears as a ramp one cell wide.

```julia
function ou_path(rng, tend; th1 = 0.0, th2 = 4.0, th3 = 0.1)
    decay = exp(-th2 * tend / FINE); sd = th3 * sqrt((1 - decay^2) / (2 * th2))
    Y = zeros(FINE + 1); Y[1] = th1 / th2 + th3 / sqrt(2 * th2) * randn(rng)
    for k in 1:FINE
        Y[k + 1] = th1 / th2 + decay * (Y[k] - th1 / th2) + sd * randn(rng)
    end
    return Y
end

function wiener_path(rng, tend)
    W = zeros(FINE + 1)
    W[2:end] .= cumsum(sqrt(tend / FINE) .* randn(rng, FINE))
    return W
end

function poisson_path(rng, tend; rate = 5.0, jump = 0.5)
    P = zeros(FINE + 1); t = randexp(rng) / rate
    while t < tend
        P[(ceil(Int, t * FINE / tend) + 1):end] .+= jump * rand(rng)
        t += randexp(rng) / rate
    end
    return P
end
poisson_pair(rng, tend) = collect.(zip(poisson_path(rng, tend), poisson_path(rng, tend)))

lerp(a::Number, b::Number, theta) = (1 - theta) * a + theta * b
lerp(a, b, theta) = (lerp(a[1], b[1], theta), lerp(a[2], b[2], theta))

function make_W(path, tend)
    return function (t)
        s = t * FINE / tend
        i = clamp(floor(Int, s), 0, FINE - 1)
        return lerp(path[i + 1], path[i + 2], s - i)
    end
end
```

None of the models has a closed-form solution. The reference is the classical fourth order
Runge-Kutta method with one step per grid cell. The path is linear inside a cell, so the noise
at the midpoint of the cell is the average of its two end values. To check the reference it is
computed a second time with two steps per cell on the same path. The largest difference between
the two is printed with every study below, and an error is reported only if it is more than 20
times that difference.

The reference is kept at $2^{14} + 1$ equally spaced times, which include the step times of
every solve below. The error of a solve is the largest absolute difference to the reference
over the saved steps and the components of the state. The strong error is the root mean square
of that over 16 independent paths.

```julia
function reference(m, path, sub = 1)
    n = FINE * sub; h = m.tend / n; every = n ÷ NSAVE
    u = copy(m.u0); k1, k2, k3, k4, tmp = ntuple(_ -> similar(u), 5)
    out = [copy(u)]
    for j in 1:n
        c = (j - 1) ÷ sub; s = (j - 1) % sub
        wa = lerp(path[c + 1], path[c + 2], s / sub)
        wb = lerp(path[c + 1], path[c + 2], (s + 1) / sub); wm = lerp(wa, wb, 0.5)
        t = (j - 1) * h
        m.f!(k1, u, m.p, t, wa)
        @. tmp = u + h / 2 * k1
        m.f!(k2, tmp, m.p, t + h / 2, wm)
        @. tmp = u + h / 2 * k2
        m.f!(k3, tmp, m.p, t + h / 2, wm)
        @. tmp = u + h * k3
        m.f!(k4, tmp, m.p, t + h, wb)
        @. u += h / 6 * (k1 + 2 * k2 + 2 * k3 + k4)
        j % every == 0 && push!(out, copy(u))
    end
    return out
end

gap(a, b) = maximum(maximum(abs, x .- y) for (x, y) in zip(a, b))

function fitted_slope(ns, errs)
    x = log2.(1 ./ ns); y = log2.(errs); n = length(x)
    return (n * sum(x .* y) - sum(x) * sum(y)) / (n * sum(x .^ 2) - sum(x)^2)
end

model(name, f!, p, u0, tend, noise, taylor) = (; name, f!, p, u0, tend, noise, taylor,
    grid = collect(range(0.0, tend; length = FINE + 1)))

const ODE_ALGS = ["Euler" => Euler(), "Tsit5" => Tsit5(), "Vern9" => Vern9()]
rode_algs(m) = vcat(["RandomEM" => RandomEM(), "RandomHeun" => RandomHeun()],
    m.taylor ? ["RandomTaylor15" => RandomTaylor15()] : [])
method_names(m) = vcat(first.(rode_algs(m)), first.(ODE_ALGS))

function setups(m, path)
    rode = RODEProblem{true}(m.f!, copy(m.u0), (0.0, m.tend), m.p,
        noise = NoiseGrid(m.grid, path))
    Wf = make_W(path, m.tend)
    ode = ODEProblem{true}((du, u, p, t) -> m.f!(du, u, p, t, Wf(t)), copy(m.u0),
        (0.0, m.tend), m.p)
    return vcat([name => (rode, alg) for (name, alg) in rode_algs(m)],
        [name => (ode, alg) for (name, alg) in ODE_ALGS])
end

run_fixed(prob, alg, dt) = solve(prob, alg, dt = dt, adaptive = false, save_everystep = true).u

function strong_study(m)
    rng = MersenneTwister(SEED)
    E = Dict(name => zeros(PATHS, length(STEPS)) for name in method_names(m))
    changes = zeros(PATHS); lo = Inf; hi = -Inf
    for i in 1:PATHS
        path = m.noise(rng, m.tend)
        lo, hi = min(lo, minimum(minimum, path)), max(hi, maximum(maximum, path))
        ref = reference(m, path)
        changes[i] = gap(ref, reference(m, path, 2))
        for (name, (prob, alg)) in setups(m, path), (j, n) in enumerate(STEPS)
            E[name][i, j] = gap(run_fixed(prob, alg, m.tend / n), ref[1:(NSAVE ÷ n):end])
        end
    end
    change = maximum(changes); limit = 20 * change
    errs = Dict(name => sqrt.(vec(mean(E[name] .^ 2, dims = 1))) for name in method_names(m))
    slopes = Dict(name => fitted_slope(STEPS[e .> limit], e[e .> limit])
    for (name, e) in errs)
    return (; errs, slopes, change, limit, lo, hi)
end

row(m, head, cells) = println(rpad(head, 7),
    join(rpad.(cells, max.(10, length.(method_names(m)) .+ 1))))
shown(e, limit) = e > limit ? @sprintf("%.3g", e) : "-"

function print_strong(m, res)
    names = method_names(m)
    @printf("%s: %d paths, T = %g, initial state %s\n", m.name, PATHS, m.tend,
        round.(m.u0, digits = 1))
    @printf("noise between %.3g and %.3g\n", res.lo, res.hi)
    @printf("two steps per cell change the reference by at most %.3g\n", res.change)
    @printf("errors below %.3g are not reported\n\n", res.limit)
    row(m, "n", names)
    for (j, n) in enumerate(STEPS)
        row(m, n, [shown(res.errs[name][j], res.limit) for name in names])
    end
    row(m, "slope", [@sprintf("%.2f", res.slopes[name]) for name in names])
end

function series!(plt, name, x, y)
    ode = name in first.(ODE_ALGS)
    return plot!(plt, x, y, label = name, linestyle = ode ? :dash : :solid,
        marker = ode ? :square : name == "RandomTaylor15" ? :diamond : :circle)
end

function convergence_plot(m, res)
    dts = m.tend ./ STEPS
    plt = plot(xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10, xlabel = "dt", ylabel = "strong error",
        title = m.name, legend = :outerright, size = (760, 420))
    for name in method_names(m)
        ok = res.errs[name] .> res.limit
        series!(plt, name, dts[ok], res.errs[name][ok])
    end
    plot!(plt, dts, dts .* (res.errs["RandomEM"][end] / dts[end]),
        linestyle = :dot, color = :black, label = "slope 1")
    if m.taylor
        ok = res.errs["RandomTaylor15"] .> res.limit; j = findlast(ok)
        plot!(plt, dts[ok], dts[ok] .^ 2 .* (res.errs["RandomTaylor15"][j] / dts[j]^2),
            linestyle = :dashdot, color = :black, label = "slope 2")
    end
    return plt
end
```

## Population dynamics

Section 15.2 of the book randomises a logistic growth model by an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process
$Y$ inside a cosine,

$$\frac{dX}{dt} = r X (k - X) \cos(Y_t), \qquad
dY_t = (\theta_1 - \theta_2 Y_t)\,dt + \theta_3\,dW_t,$$

with $X(0) = 0.5$, $r = 5$, $k = 3$, $\theta_1 = 0$, $\theta_2 = 4$ and $\theta_3 = 0.1$. Two
things are choices of this page: the end time $T = 1$, and the start of the noise, where $Y(0)$
is drawn from the stationary distribution of the process.

```julia
function population!(du, u, p, t, Y)
    du[1] = p.r * u[1] * (p.k - u[1]) * cos(Y)
    return nothing
end

population = model("Population dynamics", population!, (r = 5.0, k = 3.0), [0.5], 1.0,
    ou_path, true)
population_res = strong_study(population)
print_strong(population, population_res)
```

The printed range of the noise shows that $\cos(Y)$ stays within one percent of 1 on these
paths, so the model is a small perturbation of the deterministic logistic equation. That is why
`RandomHeun` reaches order 2 here, as it would without noise, and stays close to
`RandomTaylor15` until the finest steps, and why `Tsit5` and `Vern9` start from far smaller
errors than the RODE solvers. Their slopes are still close to 1, like those of `RandomEM` and
`Euler`, which print the same errors.

```julia
convergence_plot(population, population_res)
```

## Toggle switch

Section 15.3 takes two genes whose protein concentrations $x$ and $y$ activate themselves and
repress each other,

$$\frac{dx}{dt} = \left(\alpha_x(t) + \frac{x^4}{a^4 + x^4}\right)\frac{b^4}{b^4 + y^4}
- \lambda_x x, \qquad
\frac{dy}{dt} = \left(\alpha_y(t) + \frac{y^4}{c^4 + y^4}\right)\frac{d^4}{d^4 + x^4}
- \lambda_y y,$$

with thresholds $a = c = 0.25$ and $b = d = 0.4$, decay rates $\lambda_x = \lambda_y = 1.25$
and $x(0) = y(0) = 10$. The external activations $\alpha_x$ and $\alpha_y$ are two independent
compound Poisson processes that start at zero, with rate 5 and jump sizes uniform on
$[0, 0.5]$. The end time $T = 4$ is a choice of this page. `RandomTaylor15` takes a scalar
noise grid and the noise here has two components, so it is left out for this model.

```julia
function toggle!(du, u, p, t, alpha)
    x4 = u[1]^4; y4 = u[2]^4
    du[1] = (alpha[1] + x4 / (p.a^4 + x4)) * p.b^4 / (p.b^4 + y4) - p.lx * u[1]
    du[2] = (alpha[2] + y4 / (p.c^4 + y4)) * p.d^4 / (p.d^4 + x4) - p.ly * u[2]
    return nothing
end

toggle = model("Toggle switch", toggle!,
    (a = 0.25, b = 0.4, c = 0.25, d = 0.4, lx = 1.25, ly = 1.25), [10.0, 10.0], 4.0,
    poisson_pair, false)
toggle_res = strong_study(toggle)
print_strong(toggle, toggle_res)
```

Every method measures an order close to 1 on these paths with jumps. The classical orders 5 and
9 do not show, and the methods differ in the constant only. `Vern9` has the smallest error at
every step count, and `RandomHeun`, with two right-hand side evaluations per step, is below
`Tsit5`, with six, at every step count.

```julia
convergence_plot(toggle, toggle_res)
```

## Tumor inhibition

Section 15.1 uses a model of cancer cells $C$, promoters of their growth $P$ and inhibitors
$I$. The constant input of inhibitor is replaced by a bounded function of a Wiener process $W$:

$$\frac{dC}{dt} = \frac{rC}{\varepsilon C + 1}\,\frac{P}{I + 1} - \mu C, \qquad
\frac{dP}{dt} = a_P C - b_P P, \qquad
\frac{dI}{dt} = \zeta(W_t) + a_I C - b_I I,$$

$$\zeta(w) = \zeta_0 \left(1 - \frac{2 \nu w}{1 + w^2}\right).$$

The parameters are $r = 1$, $\mu = 0.1$, $a_P = 4.5$, $b_P = 0.11$, $a_I = 0.2$, $b_I = 0.01$,
$\varepsilon = 0.34$, $\zeta_0 = 4$ and $\nu = 1$. The initial state is $C(0) = 35$ with
$P(0) = a_P C(0) / b_P$ and $I(0) = (\zeta_0 + a_I C(0)) / b_I$, the values at which the last
two equations are at rest for $\zeta = \zeta_0$. The end time $T = 20$ is a choice of this page.

```julia
function tumor!(du, u, p, t, W)
    C, P, I = u
    du[1] = p.r * C / (p.eps * C + 1) * P / (I + 1) - p.mu * C
    du[2] = p.aP * C - p.bP * P
    du[3] = p.zeta0 * (1 - 2 * p.nu * W / (1 + W^2)) + p.aI * C - p.bI * I
    return nothing
end

tp = (r = 1.0, mu = 0.1, aP = 4.5, bP = 0.11, aI = 0.2, bI = 0.01, eps = 0.34, zeta0 = 4.0,
    nu = 1.0)
tumor = model("Tumor inhibition", tumor!, tp,
    [35.0, tp.aP / tp.bP * 35.0, (tp.zeta0 + tp.aI * 35.0) / tp.bI], 20.0, wiener_path, true)
tumor_res = strong_study(tumor)
print_strong(tumor, tumor_res)
```

The errors are absolute, and the printed initial state shows the size of the components. The
five methods that read the path only at points measure order 1, and `Tsit5` is above `Euler`
at every step count. `RandomTaylor15` measures about 1.75, and from `n = 128` on its error is
below that of every other method. Its slope is below 2 because the coarsest steps are not yet
in the asymptotic range: the method differences the right-hand side in $W$ over a distance
$\sqrt{dt}$, which is 0.8 at `n = 32`, and $\zeta$ varies on a scale of 1.

```julia
convergence_plot(tumor, tumor_res)
```

## Work-precision

This section measures error against time for the first path of each model. Each problem is
built once, outside the timer, and the time is the minimum over BenchmarkTools samples. With
DiffEqNoiseProcess 5.36.4, which this folder pins, a solve shares the stored grid of a
`NoiseGrid` and does not copy it, so the times of `RandomEM` and `RandomHeun` are the cost of
stepping.

`RandomTaylor15` computes its step integrals from every stored point of the path, so one solve
reads all $2^{20}$ points once whatever the step count. That puts a floor under its time which
is proportional to the length of the stored path. Its time at `n = 64`, where the stepping
itself costs little, is close to that floor. The step counts here run to 16384, further than in
the convergence studies, so that the order-1 methods get to spend as much time, and the line
under each table says from which step count `RandomEM` takes longer than that.

```julia
function timed_study(m)
    path = m.noise(MersenneTwister(SEED), m.tend)
    ref = reference(m, path)
    change = gap(ref, reference(m, path, 2))
    errs = Dict(name => zeros(length(WP_STEPS)) for name in method_names(m))
    times = Dict(name => zeros(length(WP_STEPS)) for name in method_names(m))
    for (name, (prob, alg)) in setups(m, path), (j, n) in enumerate(WP_STEPS)
        dt = m.tend / n
        errs[name][j] = gap(run_fixed(prob, alg, dt), ref[1:(NSAVE ÷ n):end])
        times[name][j] = @belapsed run_fixed($prob, $alg, $dt) seconds = 0.5 evals = 1
    end
    return (; errs, times, change, limit = 20 * change)
end

function print_timed(m, wp)
    names = method_names(m)
    @printf("%s, one path\n", m.name)
    @printf("two steps per cell change the reference by %.3g\n", wp.change)
    @printf("errors below %.3g are not reported\n\ntime in seconds\n", wp.limit)
    row(m, "n", names)
    for (j, n) in enumerate(WP_STEPS)
        row(m, n, [@sprintf("%.3g", wp.times[name][j]) for name in names])
    end
    println("\nerror")
    row(m, "n", names)
    for (j, n) in enumerate(WP_STEPS)
        row(m, n, [shown(wp.errs[name][j], wp.limit) for name in names])
    end
    if m.taylor
        base = wp.times["RandomTaylor15"][1]; j = findfirst(>(base), wp.times["RandomEM"])
        @printf("\nRandomTaylor15 at n=%d takes %.0f times as long as RandomEM at n=%d\n",
            WP_STEPS[1], base / wp.times["RandomEM"][1], WP_STEPS[1])
        println("RandomEM takes longer than that ",
            j === nothing ? "at no step count run here" : "from n=$(WP_STEPS[j])")
    end
end

function wp_plot(m, wp)
    plt = plot(xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10, xlabel = "time (s)", ylabel = "error",
        title = m.name * ", single path", legend = :outerright, size = (760, 420))
    for name in method_names(m)
        ok = wp.errs[name] .> wp.limit
        series!(plt, name, wp.times[name][ok], wp.errs[name][ok])
    end
    return plt
end

population_wp = timed_study(population)
print_timed(population, population_wp)
```

```julia
wp_plot(population, population_wp)
```

```julia
toggle_wp = timed_study(toggle)
print_timed(toggle, toggle_wp)
```

```julia
wp_plot(toggle, toggle_wp)
```

```julia
tumor_wp = timed_study(tumor)
print_timed(tumor, tumor_wp)
```

```julia
wp_plot(tumor, tumor_wp)
```

On the population model the noise is too small for the path between the points to matter.
`RandomHeun` has about the same error as `RandomTaylor15` in less time, and `Vern9` has the
lowest error at every step count.

On the toggle switch `Vern9` has the lowest error at every step count and the longest time, and
`RandomHeun` is below `RandomEM` throughout.

On the tumor model the floor is paid back. `RandomTaylor15` has the lowest error from `n = 256`
on, and at the finest step count it takes about as long as `Vern9` for an error more than an
order of magnitude smaller. For a coarse answer in less time than the floor, `Vern9` is the
cheaper choice.

## What this means for choosing a solver

The three models cover three situations. Where the noise barely moves the right-hand side, as
in the population model, a classical high-order method has the smallest error, `RandomHeun`
reaches order 2, and reading the path inside the step buys nothing. Where the path jumps, as in
the toggle switch, every method is order 1 and the constants decide. Where a Wiener path enters
the right-hand side at full strength, as in the tumor model, the methods that read the path at
points are held at order 1 and `RandomTaylor15` is not: past its time floor it gives the
smallest error for the time spent.

`Euler` on the interpolated path prints the same errors as `RandomEM` in every table.

```julia, echo = false
using SciMLBenchmarks
SciMLBenchmarks.bench_footer(WEAVE_ARGS[:folder], WEAVE_ARGS[:file])
```
